00:03:14.582 --> 00:03:17.791
We begin our journey at the Keflavík airport

00:03:18.457 --> 00:03:20.499
this is where we pickup our rental car.

00:03:21.124 --> 00:03:25.291
and head into one of Iceland’s 
most popular areas, the Golden Circle.

00:03:26.791 --> 00:03:29.749
Our first stop is Þingvellir National Park.

00:03:29.916 --> 00:03:32.874
Þingvellir is one of the most important places in Iceland.

00:03:33.666 --> 00:03:38.207
Historically, this is where Iceland’s 
old parliament was first established.

00:03:38.332 --> 00:03:43.124
But geologically, it’s also special place, 
because the park sits in a rift valley

00:03:43.124 --> 00:03:46.666
between the North American 
and Eurasian tectonic plates,

00:03:46.666 --> 00:03:51.252
where the land is slowly being pulled apart.

00:03:58.249 --> 00:04:01.541
From Þingvellir, we continue deeper into the Golden Circle,

00:04:01.749 --> 00:04:03.666
and make a stop at Hrunalaug.

00:04:10.082 --> 00:04:13.541
Hrunalaug is a scenic but small natural hot spring.

00:04:13.832 --> 00:04:16.776
Compared to some of the bigger
geothermal baths in Iceland,

00:04:17.053 --> 00:04:19.053
it feels much more simple and rustic.

00:04:19.832 --> 00:04:24.041
Personally, I really enjoy these smaller, 
less developed hot springs,

00:04:24.041 --> 00:04:26.541
and Hrunalaug is simply a beautiful place

00:04:26.541 --> 00:04:28.541
to stop for a quick dip.

00:04:45.916 --> 00:04:49.201
The name Gullfoss which 
translates to the “Golden Falls,”

00:04:49.201 --> 00:04:51.846
is one of Iceland’s most famous waterfalls,

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:54.707
and where the Golden Circle
gets its name from.

00:04:57.416 --> 00:05:01.957
What makes it impressive is the way the water 
drops in two stages into a deep canyon.

00:05:02.707 --> 00:05:08.082
It’s powerful, loud, and on a good day, 
you might even see rainbows forming in the mist.

00:05:10.166 --> 00:05:14.082
Nearby Gullfoss, you can also find 
the Geysir geothermal area.

00:05:14.332 --> 00:05:17.124
The original Great Geysir is mostly inactive today,

00:05:17.541 --> 00:05:19.873
but right next to it you’ll find Strokkur,

00:05:19.873 --> 00:05:23.655
which erupts every few minutes, 
shooting boiling water into the air.

00:05:27.374 --> 00:05:30.707
On our second day in Iceland, 
we head into the Highlands,

00:05:31.166 --> 00:05:34.374
following the Kjölur route to Kerlingarfjöll

00:05:40.749 --> 00:05:44.207
The drive itself is a big part 
of experiencing the Highlands.

00:05:44.541 --> 00:05:49.166
As we leave behind the more familiar surroundings, 
the landscape slowly opens up

00:05:49.332 --> 00:05:55.499
into vast stretches of gravel road, mountains, 
rivers, and wide open views.

00:05:58.374 --> 00:06:01.874
Kerlingarfjöll is a mountain range 
in the Icelandic Highlands,

00:06:04.499 --> 00:06:09.582
The area is especially known for Hveradalir, 
which means “Valley of Hot Springs.

00:06:17.291 --> 00:06:24.166
It’s a geothermal valley with colorful rhyolite mountains, steaming vents, and bubbling mud pools.

00:06:28.999 --> 00:06:33.291
You can find several hiking trails 
that will take you through the unique landscape.

00:06:58.541 --> 00:07:03.291
Near the valley, there’s a hidden hot spring 
tucked away along the river canyon,

00:07:04.082 --> 00:07:06.791
reached by a short hike from the Highland Base.

00:07:08.582 --> 00:07:15.166
The man made pool was pleasantly warm, and I honestly couldn’t believe I had this entire little spot all to myself

00:07:17.332 --> 00:07:21.041
For an alternative hot pool, 
you can visit Hveravellir,

00:07:21.041 --> 00:07:25.916
a nearby geothermal area, but the main reason 
to stop here is for the hot spring.

00:07:28.749 --> 00:07:32.416
The natural hot pool is fed by 
both hot and cold streams,

00:07:32.749 --> 00:07:36.249
so depending on where you sit, 
the temperature can change quite a bit,

00:07:36.707 --> 00:07:39.999
but it’s generally warm and 
comfortable enough for bathing.

00:07:50.499 --> 00:07:54.262
On day 3, we continue towards 
one of Iceland’s most beautiful

00:07:54.262 --> 00:07:57.060
and unique areas Landmannalaugar.

00:08:05.832 --> 00:08:08.749
On the way, we make a stop at Háifoss.

00:08:13.874 --> 00:08:18.666
Plunging 122 meters, or roughly 400 feet, 
into the canyon below,

00:08:18.874 --> 00:08:22.249
Háifoss is one of the tallest waterfalls in Iceland.

00:08:28.999 --> 00:08:32.041
The majority of visitors stay at the top viewpoint.

00:08:32.499 --> 00:08:38.124
But if you have the time, I’d recommend walking down 
into the canyon to get a closer perspective.

00:08:41.457 --> 00:08:45.832
This is where you can really feel 
the scale and force of the waterfall.

00:08:52.207 --> 00:08:57.207
We continue towards Landmannalaugar, 
following the F208 into the Highlands.

00:08:58.916 --> 00:09:03.249
Some of the most unique landscapes 
can be found along this scenic route.

00:09:03.249 --> 00:09:06.499
Along the way, you’ll pass large crater lakes,

00:09:07.624 --> 00:09:10.124
moss-covered volcanic landscapes,

00:09:10.999 --> 00:09:17.124
and fields of black volcanic sand,
stretching endlessly across the horizon.

00:10:14.832 --> 00:10:16.791
As we approach Landmannalaugar

00:10:17.249 --> 00:10:20.166
there are two river crossings 
before reaching the campsite.

00:10:24.291 --> 00:10:26.374
If you’re not comfortable with river crossings,

00:10:26.707 --> 00:10:32.582
you can park your car at the parking area before the river crossing, and simply walk the rest of the way.

00:10:32.957 --> 00:10:38.499
River crossings can be risky, so only attempt them 
if you’re confident about the depth,

00:10:38.707 --> 00:10:41.332
the current, and the safest route across.

00:10:42.041 --> 00:10:46.457
My advice is to check the river depth 
on foot if you’re unsure.

00:10:46.582 --> 00:10:50.916
Generally, you want to stay closer to the sides 
or follow the markers when possible,

00:10:51.249 --> 00:10:56.541
as rivers are often deepest in the center 
or where the water looks the calmest.

00:11:03.249 --> 00:11:06.774
Landmannalaugar is known 
for its colorful rhyolite mountains,

00:11:06.774 --> 00:11:09.081
lava fields, and hiking trails.

00:11:09.666 --> 00:11:13.749
For many, this area is one of the highlights 
of the Icelandic Highlands.

00:11:23.374 --> 00:11:26.249
If you only have time for 
one proper hike in Landmannalaugar,

00:11:26.666 --> 00:11:32.291
I would recommend doing the loop that combines 
Mount Brennisteinsalda and Bláhnúkur.

00:11:41.707 --> 00:11:47.416
The hiking loop is around 9 to 10 kilometers and takes 
most people around 4 to 5 hours to complete,

00:11:47.749 --> 00:11:49.916
and offers some of the best views in the area.

00:12:20.832 --> 00:12:25.541
And after all that hiking, you can soak in 
the natural hot spring near the campsite.

00:12:39.332 --> 00:12:44.749
On Day 4, we move out of the highlands 
and make our way towards the southern coast.

00:12:50.041 --> 00:12:54.541
We drive along the F225, 
also known as Landmannaleið.

00:12:54.874 --> 00:12:59.416
This is another beautiful Highland road, 
with incredible wide open landscapes,

00:12:59.874 --> 00:13:03.582
river crossings, and moss covered 
mountains along the way.

00:13:06.957 --> 00:13:13.166
We stop at Rauðaskál, a red volcanic crater 
that stands out for its deep red color.

00:13:22.707 --> 00:13:26.082
As we exit the Highlands, 
we continue along the South Coast.

00:13:26.249 --> 00:13:30.041
This is where our route connects with 
some of Iceland’s most famous sights,

00:13:30.291 --> 00:13:31.999
beginning with Seljalandsfoss.

00:13:32.416 --> 00:13:34.791
one of Iceland’s most iconic waterfalls,

00:13:39.666 --> 00:13:43.707
It's probably best known for the walking path 
that takes you behind the waterfall.

00:13:56.749 --> 00:14:00.791
Nearby you can also find 
this hidden waterfall inside a cave.

00:14:17.124 --> 00:14:20.749
Skógafoss is another one of Iceland’s 
most famous waterfalls,

00:14:20.749 --> 00:14:24.749
and it’s one of the most powerful 
and impressive stops along the South Coast.

00:14:27.124 --> 00:14:30.666
Close to Skógafoss, I would also 
make time for Kvernufoss.

00:14:30.874 --> 00:14:34.124
A smaller waterfall 
tucked away in a nearby canyon.

00:14:34.541 --> 00:14:37.249
It also has a path that 
takes you behind the waterfall.

00:14:44.874 --> 00:14:49.499
We continue east towards Vík, 
with a few stops along the way.

00:14:50.707 --> 00:14:56.041
Dyrhólaey is a dramatic coastal viewpoint known 
for its cliffs, black sand beaches,

00:14:56.291 --> 00:14:58.874
sea arches, and views of the coastline.

00:15:14.041 --> 00:15:19.707
From Dyrhólaey, it’s a short drive to Reynisfjara, 
Iceland’s most famous black sand beach.

00:15:20.166 --> 00:15:25.832
It’s known for its basalt columns, 
sea stacks, and powerful Atlantic waves.

00:15:37.624 --> 00:15:40.117
We then end the day in the village of Vík

00:15:40.117 --> 00:15:43.469
and grab some well-deserved 
dinner at Smiðjan Brugghús.

00:15:57.874 --> 00:16:00.874
Early morning, we head into Thakgil.

00:16:03.332 --> 00:16:07.582
Þakgil is a small canyon area 
tucked away in the mountains.

00:16:08.166 --> 00:16:11.082
The drive here already feels 
like part of the experience,

00:16:11.082 --> 00:16:13.697
with a rough gravel road 
taking you through green hills,

00:16:13.697 --> 00:16:16.158
cliffs, and wide open landscapes.

00:16:19.707 --> 00:16:24.166
Once you arrive, you’ll find a beautiful campsite 
surrounded by steep canyon walls,

00:16:24.166 --> 00:16:27.541
with several hiking trails 
leading deeper into the area.

00:16:40.832 --> 00:16:45.462
One of the best hikes here is a loop
that combines the yellow Mælifell trail

00:16:45.462 --> 00:16:47.954
and the red Austurafréttur trail.

00:16:49.291 --> 00:16:54.291
It takes around 4 to 5 hours to complete, 
and along the way, you get a mix of canyon views,

00:16:54.291 --> 00:16:57.707
mountain scenery, and views 
towards the nearby glacier.

00:17:31.666 --> 00:17:35.874
We then head out of Thakgil, 
and continue east towards Laki.

00:17:38.957 --> 00:17:42.416
On the way to Laki, we make a stop 
at this well-known canyon.

00:18:01.207 --> 00:18:04.041
The road to Laki follows F206.

00:18:04.541 --> 00:18:10.707
It’s a rough Highland route with gravel tracks,
 moss-covered rocks, and several river crossings.

00:18:23.332 --> 00:18:29.791
Laki is a volcanic area known for its long row 
of craters and moss covered lava fields.

00:18:30.791 --> 00:18:38.124
The eruption here in 1783 was one of the largest and most devastating volcanic eruptions in Iceland’s history.

00:18:39.249 --> 00:18:43.166
Today, the area feels quiet and almost untouched.

00:18:45.374 --> 00:18:49.707
You can drive along the Laki Loop that takes you 
through green moss-covered lava fields,

00:18:50.207 --> 00:18:53.082
crater landscapes, and viewpoints 
where you can really see

00:18:53.082 --> 00:18:56.041
how large this volcanic system truly is.

00:18:59.457 --> 00:19:03.457
As you exit the Laki loop, 
there is one river crossing to be aware of.

00:19:03.666 --> 00:19:06.249
Depending on the weather, this river can be deep.

00:19:06.249 --> 00:19:12.499
So make sure to ask the stationed ranger in Laki 
about the river condition before you attempt to cross it.

00:19:24.832 --> 00:19:29.249
On Day 6, we begin at Skaftafell National Park.

00:19:35.707 --> 00:19:39.457
One of the most popular hikes here 
is the trail to Svartifoss.

00:19:45.916 --> 00:19:49.707
Svartifoss is a waterfall surrounded 
by dark basalt columns,

00:19:49.707 --> 00:19:53.749
which makes it look quite different 
from many of the other waterfalls in Iceland.

00:19:57.832 --> 00:20:01.874
From Svartifoss we continue 
towards a glacier viewpoint.

00:20:11.832 --> 00:20:15.916
From the viewpoint, you get an amazing view 
of the nearby Skaftafell glacier

00:20:15.916 --> 00:20:18.374
stretching down from the Vatnajökull ice cap.

00:20:45.249 --> 00:20:49.041
After Skaftafell, we continue towards 
the Múlagljúfur Canyon.

00:20:53.499 --> 00:20:56.374
To reach the canyon, you'll need to go on a little hike.

00:20:56.582 --> 00:21:02.041
It's a 6 kilometers round trip, and takes 
most people around 2 to 3 hours to complete.

00:21:03.791 --> 00:21:06.624
It’s arguably one of the most 
beautiful canyons in Iceland,

00:21:06.874 --> 00:21:12.332
with steep green cliffs, waterfalls, and views towards 
the surrounding mountains and glaciers.

00:22:02.791 --> 00:22:07.207
Afterwards, we make a stop 
at the Jökulsárlón Glacier Lagoon.

00:22:08.291 --> 00:22:13.457
Jökulsárlón is known for its floating icebergs, 
which break away from the nearby glacier

00:22:13.457 --> 00:22:16.541
and slowly drift through the lagoon toward the sea.

00:22:31.082 --> 00:22:34.707
Right across the road from Jökulsárlón, 
you’ll find Diamond Beach.

00:22:35.999 --> 00:22:39.207
This is where some of the icebergs 
wash up on the black sand,

00:22:39.666 --> 00:22:43.041
creating these pieces of clear ice 
scattered across the beach.

00:22:43.749 --> 00:22:47.749
Depending on the tide and conditions,
it can look different every time you visit.

00:22:57.374 --> 00:23:02.207
For our final day, we continue east
and make a stop at Vestrahorn.

00:23:03.541 --> 00:23:07.041
Vestrahorn is one of Iceland’s 
most recognizable mountains,

00:23:07.249 --> 00:23:10.707
with these sharp, jagged peaks 
rising right next to the ocean.

00:23:11.832 --> 00:23:15.382
Due to its location, 
It’s a very popular photography spot,

00:23:15.843 --> 00:23:18.580
and one of my favorite places in all of Iceland.

00:23:29.791 --> 00:23:33.999
In the same area, you can also find 
the Viking Village film set.

00:23:34.166 --> 00:23:38.416
Originally built as a movie set, but today 
you can walk around the wooden buildings

00:23:38.416 --> 00:23:40.291
and explore to your heart's content.

00:23:46.374 --> 00:23:49.832
After Vestrahorn, make 
the long way back to Reykjavik.

00:23:50.291 --> 00:23:55.207
If you got the time then consider exploring the city
and enjoy some local delicacies.

00:23:55.332 --> 00:23:59.707
Down by the old harbor, you can stop by 
The Sea Baron for their famous lobster soup.

00:24:00.332 --> 00:24:04.749
For something quick and classic, 
the famous Icelandic Hot Dogs might be of interest.

00:24:05.124 --> 00:24:09.957
Personally, I enjoyed a salmon open sandwich 
and some pastries at Sandholt.

00:24:12.832 --> 00:24:16.541
And with that, our 7-day Iceland road trip comes to an end.

00:24:18.041 --> 00:24:21.207
But before we finish, here's a break down 
of the travel budget.

00:24:21.541 --> 00:24:25.999
The final cost will depend a lot on when you visit, 
what kind of vehicle you rent,

00:24:25.999 --> 00:24:30.749
but for a solo trip with a 4x4 camper van, 
this is roughly what you can expect.

00:24:31.874 --> 00:24:37.916
If you’re traveling as two people, just double the food and campsite fees, and that should give you a good estimate.

00:24:39.999 --> 00:24:43.374
I hope this video helps you plan 
your own Iceland adventure,

00:24:43.374 --> 00:24:46.541
and gives you a better idea of 
what to expect along the way.

00:24:46.707 --> 00:24:49.916
If you have any questions, feel free to 
leave them in the comments below.

00:24:50.166 --> 00:24:51.041
This is Allan.

00:24:51.332 --> 00:24:54.082
Thanks for watching, 
and I’ll see you guys in the next one.

